---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { product } from '../data/product';
---

<Base
  title="Get mxwl"
  description="Download mxwl for Linux and macOS. Install the verified Linux AppImage with one command, download a release, or build the open-source editor yourself."
>
  <header class="page-hero container">
    <div class="eyebrow">FREE SOFTWARE / YOUR WORKSPACE</div>
    <h1>
      Make yourself
      <br />
      at home<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">Get the editor. Open a folder. Bring the tools you love.</p>
    <a class="release-pill" href={product.release}>
      <span>CURRENT RELEASE</span>
      <span>v{product.version}</span>
    </a>
  </header>
  <section class="container">
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <div>
        <h2>Linux. One command.</h2>
        <p>
          The installer downloads and verifies the x86_64 AppImage, adds <code>mxwl</code> to{' '}
          <code>~/.local/bin</code>, and installs the desktop launcher and icons.
        </p>
        <p>
          Re-run the command to update. Requires <code>curl</code> and <code>sha256sum</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock code={product.installer} label="Linux / x86_64" />
    </div>
    <div class="notice">
      <span class="mono">EARLY ALPHA</span>
      <p>
        mxwl is still taking shape. Linux is the primary platform; macOS packages are ad-hoc signed
        and not notarized. There’s no auto-updater yet. Read the{' '}
        <a href="/docs/alpha/">current limitations</a> before putting it into daily use.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section">
    <div class="eyebrow">PICK YOUR PACKAGE</div>
    <div class="download-grid">
      <article class="download-card">
        <div class="eyebrow">
          <Icon name="terminal" size={18} />
          PRIMARY PLATFORM
        </div>
        <h2>Linux x86_64</h2>
        <p>AppImage for a portable install, or a .deb for Debian and Ubuntu.</p>
        <a class="button primary" href={`${product.downloads}/mxwl-${product.version}.AppImage`}>
          <Icon name="download" size={17} />
          Download AppImage
        </a>
        <a
          class="button secondary"
          href={`${product.downloads}/mxwl-editor_${product.version}_amd64.deb`}
        >
          Download .deb
        </a>
        <div class="meta">Primary / best tested</div>
      </article>
      <article class="download-card">
        <div class="eyebrow">
          <Icon name="code" size={18} />
          APPLE SILICON
        </div>
        <h2>macOS ARM64</h2>
        <p>A zip for Apple Silicon Macs. Extract it and move mxwl into Applications.</p>
        <a
          class="button primary"
          href={`${product.downloads}/mxwl-${product.version}-arm64-mac.zip`}
        >
          <Icon name="download" size={17} />
          Download ARM64 zip
        </a>
        <div class="meta">Ad-hoc signed / not notarized</div>
      </article>
      <article class="download-card">
        <div class="eyebrow">
          <Icon name="code" size={18} />
          INTEL MAC
        </div>
        <h2>macOS x64</h2>
        <p>A zip for Intel Macs. Extract it and move mxwl into Applications.</p>
        <a class="button primary" href={`${product.downloads}/mxwl-${product.version}-mac.zip`}>
          <Icon name="download" size={17} />
          Download x64 zip
        </a>
        <div class="meta">Ad-hoc signed / not notarized</div>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:24px;font-size:.875rem">
      <a class="text-link" href={`${product.downloads}/SHA256SUMS`}>
        Release checksums
      </a>{' '}
      &nbsp;{' '}
      <a class="text-link" href={product.release}>
        All release assets & notes
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section section-divider">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">FROM INSTALLED TO IN CONTEXT</div>
        <h2>
          Three steps.
          <br />
          One workspace.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Your project is the shared setup. The host is where it lives. The workspace is where you get
        to work.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="step-list">
      <div>
        <h3>Create a project.</h3>
        <p>
          Open Projects. Add your app and configure browser profiles, services, and the tools you
          want to use.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Configure the checkout.</h3>
        <p>Set paths on This machine, or add an SSH or Tailscale host for a remote checkout.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Open a workspace.</h3>
        <p>
          Choose a folder. The browser, editor, terminals, and agent are ready to share the same
          context.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <a class="text-link" href="/docs/getting-started/">
      Walk through the first launch
    </a>
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section section-divider">
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">BUILT IN THE OPEN</div>
        <h2>Prefer the source?</h2>
        <p>
          You need Node.js 20+, npm, Git, and native build tools for <code>node-pty</code>. Windows
          is buildable as a portable executable and is lightly tested.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${product.repository}#build-from-source`}>
          Full build & packaging instructions
        </a>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock
        code={`git clone ${product.repository}.git\ncd mxwl-editor\nnpm install\nnpm run dev`}
        label="Build from source"
      />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section section-divider faq">
    <div class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU GET STARTED</div>
    <h2>A few good questions.</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Is mxwl free?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. The editor is open source under the MIT license. Your agent provider’s subscriptions or
        API usage are separate.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Which coding agents work with it?</summary>
      <p>
        Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini, Kimi, Copilot, Qwen, OpenCode, Goose, and custom ACP
        agents. You authenticate with the provider’s tooling on the workspace host. See the{' '}
        <a href="/docs/agents/">agent guide</a>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I work over SSH?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. Files are browsed and edited over SFTP, while terminals, services, and agents run on
        the remote host. You can also discover and connect to Tailscale machines.{' '}
        <a href="/remote/">Explore remote workspaces</a>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Will the app keep work running when I close the window?</summary>
      <p>
        With background runtime enabled, the app stays in the system tray and keeps its live
        workspaces running. After a full restart, it restores the setup and transcripts. Use tmux
        for terminal processes that need to survive; interrupted agent tool calls don’t resume
        mid-execution.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What if I find a bug?</summary>
      <p>
        <a href={`${product.repository}/issues`}>Open a GitHub issue</a> with your OS, package type,
        local or SSH host, and steps to reproduce. Attach the app’s crash.log when relevant.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
</Base>
